# Line endings are decided here rather than by each contributor's core.autocrlf.
#
# Every blob in this repository is already stored with LF. What was missing was
# a rule for the checkout, so a Windows clone with core.autocrlf=true wrote most
# sources to disk as CRLF. That made `gofmt -l .` name about forty files as
# unformatted on Windows while the very same commit passed the Linux CI gate,
# and it made git warn about replacing LF with CRLF on almost every `git add`.
# The content was never the problem; the line endings on disk were. Pinning
# eol=lf makes the local check and the CI check agree.
#
# LF-only files suit everything this project uses: the Go toolchain, Git Bash,
# PowerShell, and the server's own file tools, which preserve whatever line
# ending a file already has whenever they edit it.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Spelled out for the types actually present, so the intent survives any later
# change to the catch-all above. Last matching rule wins.
*.go           text eol=lf
*.mod          text eol=lf
*.sum          text eol=lf
*.md           text eol=lf
*.yml          text eol=lf
*.yaml         text eol=lf
*.json         text eol=lf
*.js           text eol=lf
*.sh           text eol=lf
*.ps1          text eol=lf
Makefile       text eol=lf
.gitignore     text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf

# Never rewrite bytes that are not text. Nothing binary is tracked today, and
# tools/ (where the tunnel providers are downloaded at runtime) is ignored, but
# a stray commit of an executable or an archive should survive untouched.
*.exe   binary
*.dll   binary
*.so    binary
*.dylib binary
*.zip   binary
*.gz    binary
*.tar   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
